            <ArticleTitle>Validation of the SF-36 in patients with endometriosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>103-17</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11136-013-0442-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Endometriosis presents with significant pain as the most common symptom. Generic health measures can allow comparisons across diseases or populations. However, the Medical Outcomes Study Short Form 36 (SF-36) has not been validated for this disease. The goal of this study was to validate the SF-36 (version 2) for endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Using data from two clinical trials (N = 252 and 198) of treatment for endometriosis, a full complement of psychometric analyses was performed. Additional instruments included a pain visual analog scale (VAS); a physician-completed questionnaire based on patient interview (modified Biberoglu and Behrman--B&amp;B); clinical global impression of change (CGI-C); and patient satisfaction with treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Bodily pain (BP) and the Physical Component Summary Score (PCS) were correlated with the pain VAS at baseline and over time and the B&amp;B at baseline and end of study. In addition, those who had the greatest change in BP and PCS also reported the greatest change on CGI-C and patient satisfaction with treatment. Other subscales showed smaller, but significant, correlations with change in the pain VAS, CGI-C, and patient satisfaction with treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The SF-36--particularly BP and the PCS--appears to be a valid and responsive measure for endometriosis and its treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
